        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(sms): isolate validated webhook quotas per sender",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe SMS webhook's accepted-callback quota was keyed by resolved client address. Twilio shares egress addresses across unrelated senders, so one flooding sender could exhaust the 30/minute callback budget and make valid callbacks from other users fail with HTTP 429.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe patch keeps the existing pre-validation failed-request budget keyed by account, route, and client address, but moves the post-validation dispatch quota to account, route, and canonical sender identity.\n\n- Signature-validated callbacks use `resolveTwilioInboundSender(form)`, so equivalent SMS and `RCS:` address forms share one sender bucket.\n- A true single-sender overload remains observable as HTTP 429; the handler does not acknowledge and silently discard it.\n- Another validated sender behind the same Twilio egress address remains dispatchable.\n- With `dangerouslyDisableSignatureValidation`, `From` is unauthenticated, so the stricter callback budget stays keyed to the resolved client address and cannot be bypassed by rotating sender text.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOne abusive or bursty sender can no longer cross-throttle unrelated SMS users behind Twilio's shared infrastructure. Existing thresholds and invalid-request behavior remain unchanged, while genuine overload remains fail-closed and visible.\n\nAccepted tradeoff: removing the accidental aggregate egress-IP cap means many distinct signature-validated senders can each use their own 30/minute budget. That is intentional sender isolation; downstream pairing/access policy remains the authorization boundary.\n\n## Evidence\n\nCurrent contributor head: `16bdfc604bd` on upstream `main` `0483001ef65`.\n\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/sms/src/webhook.test.ts`: focused suite passed, including the new quota-isolation regressions.\n- Positive regression: 30 callbacks from sender A are accepted; sender B on the same egress address is still accepted.\n- Negative regression: sender A's 31st callback returns 429 even when its address changes from SMS E.164 syntax to equivalent `RCS:+1 (555) 123-4567` syntax and even when the egress IP changes.\n- Signature-disabled regression: rotating `From` values does not widen the address-keyed quota.\n- Invalid/missing canonical senders share an empty-sender bucket without consuming a valid sender's quota.\n- Fixed-window recovery regression: the sender becomes dispatchable after the window expires.\n- Direct formatter check and `git diff --check upstream/main...HEAD` passed.\n- The pre-PR plan gate passed for extension, extension-test, and docs lanes with no dependency-sensitive changes.\n\n### Real behavior proof\n\nThe focused suite drives the real request-body parser, Twilio HMAC validation, canonical sender resolver, fixed-window limiter, response writer, and durable-ingress seam with realistic HTTP requests. It proves both the intended isolation and the closest bypasses without deploying the Gateway.\n\nNo live Twilio callback was sent to this exact branch head, and no Gateway restart or production webhook change was performed. A maintainer-controlled live Twilio smoke remains the strongest optional transport proof.\n\nAI-assisted; reviewed and validated before submission.\n",
